>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

`timer_sleep()` start by calculating the timer tick at which the current
running thread should wake up by summing the current timer tick with the
ticks provided as argument to the function. The sum is then stored in a
designated `int64_t wakeup_tick` field in the thread control block, and
the thread is inserted into a global sleep list (ordered by earliest
wakeup time) and blocked. The interrupt handler is responsible for waking
it up when specified.

On each timer interrupt, the handler checks the first element of the sleep
list (the earliest) to see if its wakeup time is ≤ the current timer tick
amount. If it is, the thread is removed from the sleep list and unblocked,
and checks continue until the list is empty or there is not a thread ready
to wake up at the front.


>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The main design decision that minimizes time spent in the timer interrupt
handler is the choice to keep the sleeping thread list sorted in ascending
order of wakeup time. By doing so, slight additional cost is associated
with *adding* to the list (which occurs outside of the handler), but as
a result, there is no need to traverse the list to determine if *every*
blocked thread is ready to wake up. Instead, the first thread will always
have a wakeup time ≤ the following, so the moment the first thread entry
is determined to be unready, the handler is done for the tick.


---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

`timer_sleep()` explicitly disables interrupts before making any changes
to the shared sleep list or blocking the current thread. While some other
locking mechanism could be used, taking advantage of Pintos running only
on a single processor, disabling interrupts prevents context switches
while the operations are being performed, thus ensuring that concurrent
calls will not corrupt the sleeping thread list. Beyond the sleep list,
other data handled is not shared, so it holds no risk of race conditions.


>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

Interrupts are disabled in the critical section of `timer_sleep()`,
in which threads are added to the sleep list and blocked. Consequently,
the interrupt handler can't execute until the these operations are fully
complete, guaranteeing that the sleep list stays consistent when accessed
by the interrupt handler.


---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

The most obvious (but bad) alternative would be to insert threads to the
sleep list without ordering, which I started with before noticing that
Pintos supports `list_insert_ordered`. As described before, this design is
superior because it minimizes the time spent in the interrupt handler by
ensuring the most relevant threads are listed first and can be searched
efficiently. Knowing I had to account for race conditions, I also looked
into using Pintos locks to prevent intervention during the list management
operations. Upon realizing that Pintos is single-threaded, it was clear
that simply disabling interrupts was a cleaner strategy, since it avoids
introducing an unnecessary data structure and the complexity that comes
with it. This solution also works within the pre-existing function
content; the original code already disabled and re-enabled interrupts.


