Three fields in the `thread` struct have been added specifically for
this purpose, tied directly to the threads since current priority can be
determined uniquely for each thread. The `base_priority` field stores the
priority assigned to the thread at creation; base priority is also changed
when `thread_set_priority` is used, since it essentially acts as a
baseline for donation resolution. Base priority determines the lowest
possible priority to return to after all existing donations/dependencies
are considered. The `waiting_lock` field is used to represent the lock a
thread needs to acquire to continue execution, but that is already held by
some other thread. This is set when `acquire_lock` is called, but it is
determined that the lock is already held, and removed when the thread
leaves the semaphore wait list for the lock. `locks_held` is entirely for
the purpose of calculating thread priority after releasing a lock: since
the donated priority from that lock is now irrelevant, the thread checks
all threads dependent on the *other* locks it holds and takes the maximum
(or its `base_priority`, if larger) as its new effective `priority`.
