>> C3: Did any ambiguities in the scheduler specification make values
>> in the table uncertain?  If so, what rule did you use to resolve
>> them?  Does this match the behavior of your scheduler?

There were only a few ambiguities with the above calculations. The first
and most ignorable one was that we were not explicitly told how many ticks
occur per second. If there were, for example, 20 ticks per second, it
would have been necessary to keep track of load average in addition to
these measures for the sake of the one-second-interval recent_cpu
recalculation. This wasn't the case for priority updates, which have been
stated to occur at set 4 tick intervals. Another slight ambiguity was
whether or not the same priority maximum/minimum should be used as in this
project, but it was trivial to make the assumption that *yes*, we should.

The other ambiguity that did certainly affect output was how to handle
priority ties; it wasn't explicitly stated how to determine which thread
should run when they have the same priority. Looking at the implementation
of the Pintos `list_sort` function, it appears that it uses a form of
merge sorting that should preserve relative order given a particular
input. For that reason, I believe that breaking ties according to which
thread had the CPU most recently makes the most sense. This also makes
sense because the `priority_greater` comparator function considers on a
strict greater than basis, so equivalent values will not have their
positions swapped.


>> C4: How is the way you divided the cost of scheduling between code
>> inside and outside interrupt context likely to affect performance?

My implementation tends to more heavily emphasize scheduler work within
the timer interrupt. (In particular, incrementing `recent_cpu`, updating
all priorities and re-sorting every four ticks, and updating load average
and all recent CPU usage every second all occur within timer interrupt.)

The effect of this is that, in general, interrupt handling time will be
greater. Since these processes require iterating over the list of all
threads, timer interrupts can take significantly longer than they would
otherwise. (I locked these behaviors behind the advanced scheduler
indicator for that reason.) The cost isn't consistent either, as costly
processes fall on specific intervals, which can lead to some inconsistency
in the resume time of threads after an interrupt occurs.

This is a trade-off, however, as scheduling decisions made outside of
timer interrupts can now be made faster (or at least as fast as without
this prioritization scheme). This means that things like unblocking
threads and yielding are lightweight while the aforementioned interrupt-
based operations are sometimes slowed down heavily.


