>> C5: Briefly critique your design, pointing out advantages and
>> disadvantages in your design choices. If you were to have extra
>> time to work on this part of the project, how might you choose to
>> refine or improve your design?

My main disappointment with my own implementation is that, with each
priority recalculation every four ticks, the only effective solution I
could find in time to satisfy the scheduler was to sort the ready list in
descending order of priority. I can tell that this is not an efficient way
of going about it, but I was low on time to find an ingenious workaround
that fixed worked effectively without causing cascading issues. If I had
more time, I think I would change this method of prioritization by finding
some way to keep the ready list up to date without performing an entire
re-sort every four ticks. This contributes to the overall interrupt-
heaviness I described in C4, which I'm not proud of but also unsure how to
design around (given that actions are founded upon the current tick).

Another weakness I would go back and improve is the overall separation of
advanced scheduler handling in the `thread.c` file. It is very obviously
cluttered trying to handle 2 forms of scheduler at once within the file,
and the same goes for fields given to the `thread` struct and the
use of lines like `if (thread_mlfqs) return;` as protection. If I had more
time, I would try to separate the advanced scheduler implementation more
distinctly so that my code remained readable and effective.

My last slight regret is making relatively sparse use of the fixed-point
helper files I created for the last step. Not only did they only include
basic mathematical functions (that may as well have also been macros),
but many of the functions were never even useful in translating the useful
formulas for the scheduler. Were I to go back and revise, I would like to
implement some of the formulas themselves in these files and use them as
helpers instead of defining a bunch of static functions for that purpose
in the threads files directly.


