I prefer to stay organized, so I was highly opposed to the idea of either
hard coding each operation or creating helpers that further cluttered the
already exponentially growing "threads.c" file. For that reason, I decided
to create separate "fixed-point.h" and "fixed-point.c" helper files.

In these files, the first thing I created was the typedef `fixed_t`, named
after the only other fixed-point variables I've used in my life. While
only really an `int` beyond its name, I used this type throughout as an
indication that a particular variable or parameter was intended to be a
fixed-point integer, including the newly-added fixed-point thread fields.
The files also contain macros (used in calculations) and functions for
basic mathematical operations between fixed-point numbers and between
fixed-point and normal integers.

Doing so allowed me to express the advanced scheduler-relevant formulas as
provided in documentation without relying on excessive visible conversions
or unclear terminology. (If only operator overload was realistic, it would
be even more clear.) While the abstraction layer was helpful for
differentiating (especially for preventing accidental normal integer math
with intended fixed-point values), it was also at times excessively
verbose in terms of function names, leading to even more clutter in an
already cluttered thread code file. As I described before, I'd prefer to
clean up the integration of everything if I had more time.